-- hpe-zoom-agilent-earnings-monday-november-23-20261122: fix bodyEn
UPDATE "Article" SET "bodyEn" = $$<p>Earnings Monday November 23 brings a heavy slate of results after the US close, led by HPE, Zoom and Agilent, alongside November purchasing managers surveys in the eurozone, the UK and the US.</p><table><tbody><tr><td>Date</td><td>Monday 23 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>After the US close</td><td>Hewlett Packard Enterprise Q4, Zoom Communications Q3, Agilent Technologies Q4, Jacobs Solutions Q4, Symbotic Q4</td></tr><tr><td>Before the open</td><td>Dick's Sporting Goods Q3, Kohl's Q3, Burlington Stores Q3, Abercrombie and Fitch Q3</td></tr><tr><td>Consensus estimates</td><td>HPE 1.28 to 1.30 dollars a share on about 14.4 billion dollars revenue; Zoom 1.49 to 1.53 dollars on about 1.28 billion dollars</td></tr><tr><td>Data</td><td>November manufacturing and services PMIs for the eurozone, UK and US</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What to watch as HPE, Zoom and Agilent report</h2><p>Four AI hardware names report this week, and Hewlett Packard Enterprise kicks the sequence off after the US close on Monday, with Analog Devices before the open on Tuesday and Dell Technologies and HP after the close the same day. HPE is expected to report fourth-quarter earnings of about 1.28 to 1.30 dollars a share on revenue of about 14.4 billion dollars. Zoom Communications reports third-quarter results after the close too, with consensus at about 1.49 to 1.53 dollars a share on about 1.28 billion dollars of revenue, followed by Autodesk and Workday after the close on Tuesday. Agilent Technologies and Jacobs Solutions also report after the close on Monday. Before the open, retailers Dick's Sporting Goods, Kohl's, Burlington Stores and Abercrombie and Fitch report third-quarter results, giving an early read on US consumer spending ahead of Black Friday. In total, 26 companies report on Monday, six before the open and 19 after the close. Readers following <a href="https://growentix.cloud/usa">USA</a> markets can also read our report on <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/walmart-q3-results-revenue-188-billion-20261121">Walmart third-quarter results, with revenue of 188 billion dollars</a>.</p><h2>What earnings Monday November 23 means for investors</h2><p>The session comes after a brutal week for US stocks: on Friday 20 November the S and P 500 fell 3.7 percent to 6,910.39, its seventh straight loss, leaving it more than 10 percent below its 6 October record and in correction territory, with an 8.6 percent weekly drop, the worst since April 2025. The Nasdaq fell 5.28 percent on Friday. Against that backdrop, HPE results will be read as a test of AI hardware demand after Nvidia fell 14 percent on Friday on the SEC accounting report, and Zoom results will be read as a test of software spending. The November PMIs, due the same day in the eurozone, the UK and the US, will show whether the sell-off is matched by weakening business activity. No outcome is assumed here: these are the numbers to watch, not a forecast of what they will be.</p><h2>What the sources do not say</h2><p>Consensus estimates differ between providers: MarketBeat gives HPE at 1.28 dollars and Zoom at 1.49 dollars, while other calendars give 1.30 and 1.53 dollars, so this article gives both figures as a range. The sources do not say what guidance any company will give, and earnings dates can move, so readers should check the live calendar before trading around any report. Nothing in this article predicts whether any stock will rise or fall.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://www.stockopedia.com/content/the-week-ahead-23-27-nov-sell-off-pmis-us-core-pce-gdp-boe-financial-stability-us-ai-hardware-us-software-cmc-markets-1061141/">Stockopedia</a>, the week ahead (Monday reporters, HPE and Zoom timing, AI hardware sequence); <a href="https://www.marketbeat.com/earnings/tomorrow/?AccountID=4041930&hash=0F4BDEC842AA0970778218EE4F75EBAF992325D902368EFB132BDBECC7EA30039E10D884BE9EE066F854DCC77F3F68EA37D364FFB802AD39260BD5D22E0F4756&utm_source=newsletter&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=newsletterclick">MarketBeat</a>, the Monday 23 November earnings list and consensus estimates; <a href="https://www.cmcmarkets.com/en-ie/news-and-analysis/the-week-ahead-global-pmis-us-pce-dell-earnings">CMC Markets</a>, the Monday 23 November data and results schedule.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Which companies report earnings on Monday 23 November?</h3><p>Hewlett Packard Enterprise, Zoom, Agilent and Jacobs report after the US close; Dick's Sporting Goods, Kohl's, Burlington and Abercrombie and Fitch report before the open.</p><h3>What time do HPE and Zoom report?</h3><p>Both report after the US market close on Monday 23 November 2026.</p><h3>What else is due on Monday 23 November?</h3><p>November manufacturing and services PMIs for the eurozone, the UK and the US.</p>$$, "updatedAt" = NOW() WHERE "slug" = 'hpe-zoom-agilent-earnings-monday-november-23-20261122';

-- huawei-mate-x8-launch-november-24-20261122: fix bodyEn
UPDATE "Article" SET "bodyEn" = $$<p>Huawei will unveil the Mate X8 foldable smartphone in China on Monday 24 November 2026, at an all-scenario launch event starting at 2:30 pm local time. The Huawei Mate X8 keeps the inward-folding layout of the Mate X line, with a new circular camera module, larger displays and five colour finishes.</p><table><tbody><tr><td>Organisation</td><td>Huawei</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Huawei Mate X8 launch: what is confirmed</h2><p>Huawei announced the 24 November event on Weibo and has since shown the phone design in official images, including Harper Bazaar editorial photographs. The Mate X8 will come in five colours: Purple Gold Brocade, Azure Brocade, Danxia Brocade, Sky Black and Snow White. Huawei official website lists the configurations: standard models with 12GB of RAM and 256GB or 512GB of storage, and Collector Edition models with 16GB of RAM and 512GB or 1TB of storage. The rear camera uses a circular design with a four-camera setup, including a 50MP main camera with variable aperture, a 40MP ultra-wide camera and a 50MP telephoto macro camera with optical image stabilisation, supporting 3.5x optical zoom and 100x digital zoom. The main display is expected to measure around 8.15 inches and the cover screen around 6.5 inches. Other devices, including the MateBook Fold S, are expected at the same event. Readers can also read our report on the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/xiaomi-18-pro-max-global-launch-20261121">Xiaomi 18 Pro Max global launch</a>, and see more phone news in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/tech-ai">Tech</a> section.</p><h2>What it means</h2><p>The Mate X8 is Huawei flagship foldable for the year, and the launch comes as foldables compete on camera hardware as much as on screens. The most talked-about detail is the Ruiying Z10 external camera module, reported to offer a 1-inch 50MP sensor with 10x optical zoom from 24mm to 240mm, which would turn the phone into a serious camera system. If the module is real and works as reported, it would be the most distinctive camera accessory on a foldable yet.</p><h2>What the sources do not say</h2><p>Huawei has not announced the price or the sale date, and has not confirmed the full specifications, the chipset or the battery size. Reports of a battery of at least 6,000mAh, the Ruiying Z10 module and its Collector Edition exclusivity are unconfirmed, and one report notes Huawei has not confirmed whether the external camera is exclusive to the Collector Edition. The sources also do not say whether the Mate X8 will be sold outside China, or when.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://www.gizmochina.com/2026/11/16/huawei-mate-x8-launch-date-set-for-november-24/">GizmoChina</a>, the 24 November launch date and the reported specifications; <a href="https://www.huaweicentral.com/huawei-mate-x8-launch-november-24/amp/">Huawei Central</a>, the official event announcement and the camera details; <a href="https://gadgets.beebom.com/news/huawei-mate-x8-foldable-china-launch-date-confirmed">Beebom</a>, the launch time and the design reveal.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>When is the Huawei Mate X8 launch?</h3><p>Monday 24 November 2026 in China, at 2:30 pm local time.</p><h3>What colours and configurations will it come in?</h3><p>Five colours: Purple Gold Brocade, Azure Brocade, Danxia Brocade, Sky Black and Snow White. Standard models offer 12GB of RAM with 256GB or 512GB of storage; Collector Edition models offer 16GB of RAM with 512GB or 1TB.</p><h3>Will the Mate X8 be sold outside China?</h3><p>Huawei has not said. The launch announced so far is for China only, and no global date has been given.</p>$$, "updatedAt" = NOW() WHERE "slug" = 'huawei-mate-x8-launch-november-24-20261122';

-- openai-gpt-live-1-1-standby-idle-cost-20261122: fix bodyEn
UPDATE "Article" SET "bodyEn" = $$<p>OpenAI GPT-Live-1.1 arrived in the API on 19 November, with a standby mode that cuts the cost of an idle voice session by 60%. GPT-Live-1.1 is a full-duplex voice model that listens while it speaks, and idle WebRTC time now bills at $0.02 per minute instead of $0.05 per minute for active inference.</p><table><tbody><tr><td>Organisation</td><td>OpenAI</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>How OpenAI GPT-Live-1.1 standby works</h2><p>Standby is a billing state for WebRTC sessions. Developers set a wake policy of speech, so inference waits for voice activity, and an idle timeout of 5 to 60 seconds, after which the session pauses inference while the WebRTC connection, the session ID and the conversation history stay open. When the user speaks, the session resumes. OpenAI sends session.paused when standby starts and session.resumed when inference restarts. The voice activity threshold runs from 0 to 1 and defaults to 0.5. Backend models and tools that GPT-Live calls through delegation are billed separately and do not change with standby. GPT-Live-1.1 also improves pronunciation, accent consistency and transcription in multilingual voice conversations, and can delegate reasoning and tool calls to a backend model while it keeps talking. Readers can also read our report on <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/openai-gpt-6-2-astra-sol-launch-20261120">OpenAI GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol launch</a>, and more AI news in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/tech-ai">Tech</a> section.</p><h2>What it means</h2><p>A voice session spends much of its life saying nothing, and until now that silence was billed at the active rate simply because the connection was open. Standby turns silence into something a developer manages, with a price, a timeout and events to count. It fits support calls, form filling, coaching and in-car assistants, where pauses are part of the conversation. The same model has powered ChatGPT Plus and Pro voice since 15 October, so the API release brings a model already in use to developers building their own voice agents.</p><h2>What the sources do not say</h2><p>OpenAI has not published benchmark numbers for the transcription or reliability gains in GPT-Live-1.1, so those improvements are vendor claims until developers measure them. The sources do not say how much slower the first reply after waking from standby may be, beyond noting that OpenAI warns it may be slower after a short utterance. They also do not say whether standby will come to WebSocket sessions, which are not covered by the new billing state.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/changelog?change=you-can-now-choose-which-service-tiers-projects-can-use-in-p">OpenAI API changelog</a>, the 19 November GPT-Live-1.1 entry and the standby configuration; <a href="https://www.marktechpost.com/2026/11/21/openai-gpt-live-1-1-api-standby-mode/">MarkTechPost</a>, the $0.02 standby rate, the 5 to 60 second timeout and the slower first reply warning; <a href="https://aiweekly.co/alerts/openai-opens-gpt-live-11-to-api-with-002min-standby">AI Weekly</a>, the API availability and the standby pricing.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What does GPT-Live-1.1 standby cost?</h3><p>Idle WebRTC time bills at $0.02 per minute, against $0.05 per minute for active inference, a 60% lower rate. Backend model and tool usage is billed separately.</p><h3>Does standby work on WebSocket sessions?</h3><p>No. Standby applies to WebRTC sessions only, and it is configured when the session is created.</p><h3>What changes when upgrading from GPT-Live-1?</h3><p>Very little. Developers switch an existing Live session by setting the model to gpt-live-1.1, with no new endpoint and no migration.</p>$$, "updatedAt" = NOW() WHERE "slug" = 'openai-gpt-live-1-1-standby-idle-cost-20261122';

-- claude-black-hole-physics-problems-solved-20261122: fix bodyEn, excerptEn
UPDATE "Article" SET "excerptEn" = 'Claude black hole physics results solved two open problems, Anthropic said on 17 November, finishing a 2003 calculation and finding an exact limit.', "bodyEn" = $$<p>Anthropic said on 17 November that Claude Fable 5.2 solved two open problems in black hole physics, including a calculation physicists set out in 2003 but never completed. The Claude black hole physics results concern theoretical universes and the search for a theory of quantum gravity, and both are published as preprints that have not yet completed independent peer review.</p><table><tbody><tr><td>Organisation</td><td>Anthropic</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What the Claude black hole physics results show</h2><p>The first problem is the Hawking-Page phase transition: what happens when a theoretical universe is heated until a gas of particles gives way to a black hole. In 2003 physicists set out the calculation that would settle whether the switch stays abrupt when gravity is weak, but it was extremely complex and never completed. Claude ran a small research group of copies of itself, worked out almost every piece of the calculation independently and checked its maths against known results, and showed that the transition is abrupt. The second problem concerns a simplified universe with two dimensions of space, where physicists use the modular bootstrap to rule out inconsistent theories of quantum gravity. Claude noticed a pattern in numerical results that physicists had missed, borrowed a method from elsewhere in mathematics and physics, and calculated the exact ceiling for the simplest version of the technique, about c divided by 9.1266, proving it could not be lowered. Readers can also read our report on <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/anthropic-ant-first-day-trading-results-20261121">Anthropic first day trading results</a>, and more AI research news in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/tech-ai">Tech</a> section.</p><h2>How Claude worked, and what it means</h2><p>In August, Anthropic researcher Hongbin Chen asked Claude to draw up a list of interesting open problems in theoretical physics, focusing on black holes and the bootstrap method. Claude returned 22 candidates, and agents spent about six weeks investigating them, reading each other progress posts and collaborating where work overlapped. For the first problem, agents repeated most of the calculations, and a physicist using Claude obtained the same answer in a different way. For the second, Anthropic reports multiple agents converging, formal Lean verification and physicist review. Anthropic says the work shows AI can contribute to complex research, though the company is explicit that neither result describes our own universe directly and neither has practical applications.</p><h2>What the sources do not say</h2><p>Neither preprint has completed independent peer review, so the results are Anthropic claims until physicists outside the company check them. The sources do not say whether the wider physics community accepts the proofs, and they do not say what the results mean for real black holes, which they do not describe. Anthropic also notes the first result covers only weak coupling, leaving intermediate interaction strengths unresolved, and the second result sets a ceiling only for the simplest bootstrap variant, not the correct theory of gravity.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/claude-black-hole-physics">Anthropic</a>, the research post describing both problems, the methods and the limits; <a href="https://www.eweek.com/news/anthropic-claude-black-hole-physics-explained/">eWeek</a>, the 17 November announcement, the six-week agent effort and the peer-review status; <a href="https://gizmodo.com/anthropics-says-its-new-ai-model-can-solve-black-hole-mysteries-what-could-go-wrong-2000837274">Gizmodo</a>, the Fable 5.2 launch context and the unreviewed status of the findings.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Has the black hole work been peer reviewed?</h3><p>No. The two results are published as preprints, and neither has completed independent peer review.</p><h3>Do the results describe real black holes?</h3><p>No. Both concern theoretical toy universes. Anthropic says neither result describes our own universe directly and neither has practical applications.</p><h3>What did Claude actually solve?</h3><p>First, it completed a 2003 Hawking-Page calculation, showing the gas-to-black-hole transition stays abrupt at weak coupling. Second, it found the exact ceiling, about c divided by 9.1266, for the simplest modular bootstrap method.</p>$$, "updatedAt" = NOW() WHERE "slug" = 'claude-black-hole-physics-problems-solved-20261122';

-- us-smartphone-shipments-fall-9-percent-q3-2026-20261122: fix bodyEn
UPDATE "Article" SET "bodyEn" = $$<p>US smartphone shipments fell 9% year on year in the third quarter of 2026, from July to September, according to Counterpoint Research Market Monitor report, as global memory constraints hit Android shipments, especially at the low end. Apple was the only major maker whose shipments grew, lifting its United States share to 57%.</p><table><tbody><tr><td>Organisation</td><td>Counterpoint Research</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What the US smartphone shipments data shows</h2><p>Apple increased its share by 7 percentage points year on year and 3 points quarter on quarter, to 57% in Q3 2026, up from 50% a year earlier. iPhone shipments rose 7% year on year, and Apple was the only original equipment maker not affected by shipment declines in the United States market. Samsung came second with 25% market share, up 1 point year on year but down 5 points from 30% in the previous quarter, with shipments up 1% year on year. Counterpoint attributed the wider contraction to sluggish demand for low-end Android devices alongside ongoing memory supply constraints that have raised component costs and tightened availability across budget tiers. Readers can also read our report on the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/google-pixel-drop-november-2026-pixel-tag-20261120">Google November Pixel Drop and Pixel Tag</a>, and more phone news in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/tech-ai">Tech</a> section.</p><h2>What it means</h2><p>The memory shortage is reshaping the United States market in favour of the premium end. Apple and Samsung were the only makers to increase their shipment shares year on year, and Samsung dual strategy of flagship models and low-end A-series devices helped it perform relatively better than other Android makers. For buyers, the same constraints point towards higher prices and tighter availability for budget phones, the segment where the fall was concentrated, while the premium segment keeps growing.</p><h2>What the sources do not say</h2><p>Counterpoint report gives shares and percentage changes, not total units shipped, so the sources do not say how many phones were actually sold in the quarter. They do not give average selling prices or a forecast for the fourth quarter, and they do not break out shares for Motorola, Google or other makers beyond Apple and Samsung. The sources also do not say how long the memory constraints will last.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://counterpointresearch.com/en/insights/us-smartphone-market-share">Counterpoint Research</a>, the Q3 2026 Market Monitor data, the 9% fall and the Apple 57% share; <a href="https://www.gadgets360.com/mobiles/news/us-smartphone-market-share-in-q3-2026-shipments-apple-samsung-motorola-google-counterpoint-12353142?from=gha">Gadgets 360</a>, the shipment figures, the Apple and Samsung shares and the memory constraint cause; <a href="https://www.sammobile.com/news/samsung-and-apple-only-oems-increase-us-phone-shipment-share-yoy-q3-2026/">SamMobile</a>, the iPhone shipment growth and the Samsung quarter-on-quarter fall.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Which brand grew in the United States in Q3 2026?</h3><p>Apple only. iPhone shipments rose 7% year on year, and Apple share reached 57%, up from 50% a year earlier.</p><h3>Why did United States smartphone shipments fall?</h3><p>Counterpoint points to global memory constraints affecting Android shipments, especially low-end models, alongside sluggish demand for low-end Android devices.</p><h3>What was Samsung share?</h3><p>25% in Q3 2026, up 1 point year on year but down 5 points from 30% in the previous quarter.</p>$$, "updatedAt" = NOW() WHERE "slug" = 'us-smartphone-shipments-fall-9-percent-q3-2026-20261122';

-- nvidia-sec-accounting-probe-huang-senate-testimony-20261122: fix bodyEn, excerptEn, titleEn
UPDATE "Article" SET "titleEn" = 'Nvidia SEC Accounting Probe Deepens as Huang Agrees to Testify', "excerptEn" = 'The Nvidia SEC accounting probe deepened as Reuters reported accounting issues under review, while Jensen Huang agreed to testify before the US Senate.', "bodyEn" = $$<p>The Nvidia SEC accounting probe deepened on Friday 20 November, as Reuters reported that SEC enforcement staff are examining possible accounting issues at the chipmaker, while former chief executive Jensen Huang agreed to testify in public before the US Senate.</p><table><tbody><tr><td>Company</td><td>Nvidia, Santa Clara, California</td></tr><tr><td>SEC matter</td><td>Enforcement staff examining possible accounting issues, per Reuters, citing two people briefed on the matter</td></tr><tr><td>Share price</td><td>Closed Friday at 125.53 dollars, down about 14 percent; down 48 percent from the 6 October record of 239.24 dollars</td></tr><tr><td>Senate hearing</td><td>Huang agreed to testify in public before the Senate Banking Committee; no date set, 3 December first requested</td></tr><tr><td>House interview</td><td>Closed-door transcribed interview on 1 December</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What happened in the Nvidia SEC accounting probe</h2><p>Reuters reported on Friday, citing two people briefed on the matter, that SEC enforcement staff are scrutinising potential accounting issues at Nvidia. The SEC declined to comment and Nvidia did not immediately respond. Reuters said an investigation is not evidence of wrongdoing, and many close without an enforcement action, and it could not determine when the inquiry started or exactly what issues are being examined. The Wall Street Journal had reported earlier in the week that the SEC had opened an investigation into Nvidia. Shares were already down more than 6 percent before the Reuters report and closed 14 percent lower at 125.53 dollars, with the Nasdaq down 5.3 percent and the S and P 500 down 3.7 percent on the day. Separately, a spokesperson for Senate Banking chair Tim Scott confirmed on Friday that Huang will testify in public before the committee. Scott first asked for 3 December and has said he wants to hear from Huang and Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick before Christmas. Readers following <a href="https://growentix.cloud/usa">USA</a> tech news can also read our report on <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/nvidia-q3-earnings-results-20261119">Nvidia third-quarter earnings results</a>.</p><h2>What the Nvidia SEC accounting probe means</h2><p>The probe adds a regulatory front to a crisis that has already cost Nvidia about 2.7 trillion dollars in market value since its 6 October record. Huang resigned as chief executive on 12 November, and the board has said it unanimously accepted his resignation and does not intend to reinstate him, even after President Trump said on Friday that Nvidia should bring him back. California Attorney General Rob Bonta served Nvidia with an investigative subpoena on Thursday, investigating whether Nvidia or Huang actions resulted in China obtaining advanced chips or confidential information, and whether the company misled investors about the risk. The Justice Department is running a criminal investigation of Megaspeed, the Singapore company at the centre of the chip diversion claims. For investors, the week ahead is now about two dates: the closed-door House interview on 1 December and the public Senate hearing, which Scott must hold before Democrats take over the committee when the new Congress meets on 3 January.</p><h2>What the sources do not say</h2><p>The sources do not say what accounting issues the SEC is examining, when the inquiry began, or whether it will lead to any enforcement action. They do not give a date for the Senate hearing, and they do not say what Huang will be asked about the accounting questions as opposed to the China chip claims. The sources do not say whether the board search for a permanent chief executive has produced a shortlist, or whether any board members are still pushing to reinstate Huang.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://www.tradingview.com/news/benzinga:02fcd6e1e094b:0-jensen-huang-set-for-public-senate-banking-hearing-as-sec-scrutinizes-nvidia-accounting-report/">TradingView</a>, the Benzinga report on the Senate testimony and the SEC accounting inquiry; <a href="https://www.sovereignmagazine.com/article/nvidia-troubles-sec-accounting">Sovereign Magazine</a>, the Reuters report, the 14 percent fall and the California subpoena; <a href="https://cosanalyst.com/articles/nvidia-jensen-huang-board-investigation-what-it-means-for-nvda-stock-2026-11-08/">Cosanalyst</a>, the Reuters and CNBC sourcing and the hearing dates.</p>$$, "updatedAt" = NOW() WHERE "slug" = 'nvidia-sec-accounting-probe-huang-senate-testimony-20261122';
